
<h2>18.9 statsmodels: OLS, GLM, ARIMA and the Formula API</h2>
<p>scikit-learn asks "how well does this predict?". statsmodels asks "what do the coefficients mean, and how sure are we?". It returns standard errors, t-statistics, p-values, confidence intervals and diagnostic tests in one summary table, which is what analysts, economists and scientists must report. <strong>statsmodels is not installed on this study machine.</strong> Each statsmodels block below is labelled <em>not run here</em>: it is standard, idiomatic code, but no output is shown for it, because I will not invent output. Directly after each one comes a NumPy/SciPy version that did run, which computes the same quantities from first principles. Run the statsmodels code yourself after <code>python3 -m pip install statsmodels</code> and compare.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does in plain English</th><th>Minimal example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>sm.add_constant(X)</code></td><td>Add the intercept column (statsmodels does not add one for you)</td><td><code>X = sm.add_constant(X)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.OLS(y, X).fit()</code></td><td>Ordinary least squares regression</td><td><code>res = sm.OLS(y, X).fit()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.summary()</code></td><td>Full table: coefficients, errors, p, R-squared</td><td><code>print(res.summary())</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.params</code>, <code>bse</code></td><td>Coefficients and their standard errors</td><td><code>res.params["x"]</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.pvalues</code>, <code>tvalues</code></td><td>Test of each coefficient being zero</td><td><code>res.pvalues</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.conf_int(alpha=0.05)</code></td><td>95% interval for each coefficient</td><td><code>res.conf_int()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.rsquared</code>, <code>rsquared_adj</code></td><td>Share of variance explained</td><td><code>res.rsquared_adj</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.resid</code>, <code>fittedvalues</code></td><td>Residuals and fitted values for diagnostics</td><td><code>res.resid.std()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.predict(X_new)</code></td><td>Predictions for new rows</td><td><code>res.predict(X_new)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.get_prediction(X).summary_frame()</code></td><td>Predictions with confidence and prediction intervals</td><td><code>pred.summary_frame(alpha=0.05)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fit(cov_type="HC3")</code></td><td>Robust standard errors when the spread is uneven</td><td><code>sm.OLS(y, X).fit(cov_type="HC3")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>smf.ols("y ~ x1 + C(g)", data=df)</code></td><td>Formula interface: column names, categories built in</td><td><code>smf.ols("price ~ size + C(city)", df).fit()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.GLM(y, X, family=...)</code></td><td>Generalised linear model (Poisson, Binomial, Gamma)</td><td><code>sm.GLM(y, X, family=sm.families.Poisson())</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.Logit(y, X)</code></td><td>Logistic regression with inference</td><td><code>sm.Logit(y, X).fit()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.stats.anova_lm(res)</code></td><td>Analysis of variance table</td><td><code>sm.stats.anova_lm(res, typ=2)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>variance_inflation_factor</code></td><td>Detect collinear features</td><td><code>variance_inflation_factor(X.values, i)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>het_breuschpagan</code></td><td>Test for uneven residual spread</td><td><code>sm.stats.diagnostic.het_breuschpagan(res.resid, X)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>durbin_watson</code></td><td>Residual autocorrelation (near 2 is fine)</td><td><code>sm.stats.durbin_watson(res.resid)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.tsa.ARIMA(y, order=(p, d, q))</code></td><td>Time-series model with trend and noise structure</td><td><code>ARIMA(y, order=(1, 0, 0)).fit()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.tsa.SARIMAX</code></td><td>ARIMA with seasonality and outside regressors</td><td><code>SARIMAX(y, order=(1,1,1), seasonal_order=(1,0,0,7))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>res.forecast(steps)</code></td><td>Predict the next steps of a time series</td><td><code>res.forecast(7)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.tsa.seasonal_decompose</code></td><td>Split into trend, season and rest</td><td><code>seasonal_decompose(y, period=7)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.tsa.adfuller</code></td><td>Test whether a series is stationary</td><td><code>adfuller(y)[1]</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.tsa.acf</code>, <code>pacf</code></td><td>Correlation of a series with its own past</td><td><code>sm.tsa.acf(y, nlags=20)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.stats.proportions_ztest</code></td><td>Compare two conversion rates</td><td><code>proportions_ztest([60, 85], [500, 500])</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.stats.multipletests</code></td><td>Adjust many p-values</td><td><code>multipletests(p, method="fdr_bh")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sm.stats.power.TTestIndPower</code></td><td>Sample size or power planning</td><td><code>TTestIndPower().solve_power(effect_size=0.3, power=0.8, alpha=0.05)</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Worked example 1: OLS with a full inference table</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.9.1</span> Regress y on x for 80 observations (true line y = 2 + 0.7 x). Report coefficients, standard errors, p-values, 95% intervals and R-squared. First the statsmodels code, which is <strong>not run here</strong>.</p>
@@NORUN
import numpy as np
import statsmodels.api as sm

rng = np.random.default_rng(11)
x = rng.uniform(0, 10, 80)
y = 2 + 0.7 * x + rng.normal(0, 1.0, 80)

X = sm.add_constant(x)            # adds a column of ones for the intercept
res = sm.OLS(y, X).fit()
print(res.summary())              # coefficients, std errors, t, p, CI, R-squared
print(res.params, res.bse, res.pvalues)
print(res.conf_int(alpha=0.05))
@@END
<ol><li><code>sm.add_constant</code> is needed: unlike scikit-learn, statsmodels fits <em>no</em> intercept unless you add the column. Forgetting it is the classic statsmodels bug.</li><li><code>sm.OLS(y, X)</code> takes the response first, then the design matrix (the reverse of scikit-learn's order for <code>fit(X, y)</code>).</li><li><code>.fit()</code> returns a results object. <code>summary()</code> prints the full table; the attributes give each part as arrays.</li></ol>
<p>The same numbers, computed by hand with NumPy and SciPy (this block ran):</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import stats

rng = np.random.default_rng(11)
x = rng.uniform(0, 10, 80)
y = 2 + 0.7 * x + rng.normal(0, 1.0, 80)

X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(x), x])
n, k = X.shape
beta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)              # normal equations
resid = y - X @ beta
sigma2 = resid @ resid / (n - k)                      # unbiased noise variance
cov = sigma2 * np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X)
se = np.sqrt(np.diag(cov))
t = beta / se
p = 2 * stats.t.sf(np.abs(t), df=n - k)
tcrit = stats.t.ppf(0.975, n - k)
r2 = 1 - resid @ resid / ((y - y.mean()) @ (y - y.mean()))

for name, b, s, tv, pv in zip(["const", "x"], beta, se, t, p):
    print("%-5s coef=%.4f se=%.4f t=%.2f p=%.2g CI=(%.4f, %.4f)" % (name, b, s, tv, pv, b - tcrit * s, b + tcrit * s))
print("R-squared = %.4f" % r2)
@@END
<ol><li>The design matrix has a ones column and the x column, exactly what <code>add_constant</code> builds.</li><li>The least-squares coefficients solve (X<sup>T</sup>X) beta = X<sup>T</sup>y. (In production prefer <code>lstsq</code>; this form keeps the algebra visible.)</li><li>The noise variance is the sum of squared residuals divided by n minus k (observations minus parameters); dividing by n would underestimate it.</li><li>The coefficient covariance matrix is that variance times the inverse of X<sup>T</sup>X. Standard errors are square roots of its diagonal.</li><li>The t-statistic is coefficient over standard error, and the two-sided p-value comes from the t distribution with n minus k degrees of freedom.</li><li>The confidence interval is coefficient plus or minus a t quantile times the standard error. statsmodels reports these same quantities in <code>params</code>, <code>bse</code>, <code>pvalues</code> and <code>conf_int()</code>.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 2: uneven spread and robust standard errors</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.9.2</span> When the noise grows with x, ordinary standard errors are unreliable. See the fan shape in the residuals, then compute heteroscedasticity-robust (HC3) standard errors. statsmodels: <code>sm.OLS(y, X).fit(cov_type="HC3")</code> (not run here).</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import stats

rng = np.random.default_rng(11)
x = rng.uniform(0, 10, 80)
y = 2 + 0.7 * x + rng.normal(0, 0.4 + 0.12 * x)
X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(x), x])
n, k = X.shape
XtX_inv = np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X)
beta = XtX_inv @ X.T @ y
resid = y - X @ beta

se_classic = np.sqrt(np.diag(resid @ resid / (n - k) * XtX_inv))
h = np.einsum("ij,jk,ik->i", X, XtX_inv, X)            # leverage of each row
w = resid / (1 - h)
meat = (X * w[:, None]).T @ (X * w[:, None])
se_hc3 = np.sqrt(np.diag(XtX_inv @ meat @ XtX_inv))
print("slope %.3f" % beta[1])
print("classic se: %.4f   robust HC3 se: %.4f" % (se_classic[1], se_hc3[1]))

fit = X @ beta
lo = np.abs(resid[fit < np.median(fit)]).mean()
hi = np.abs(resid[fit >= np.median(fit)]).mean()
print("mean |residual| low half: %.2f, high half: %.2f" % (lo, hi))
print("Spearman(fitted, |resid|) = %.2f" % stats.spearmanr(fit, np.abs(resid)).statistic)
@@END
<ol><li>The true noise standard deviation is 0.4 plus 0.12 times x, so large x values are noisier.</li><li>The classic standard error assumes equal noise everywhere.</li><li>The HC3 "sandwich": the middle part (meat) weights each row by its own squared residual divided by (1 minus leverage) squared; bread and meat are multiplied as XtX_inv, meat, XtX_inv. The leverage <code>h</code> says how unusual a row is.</li><li>Two diagnostics expose the problem: residuals are larger in the high half of fitted values, and the rank correlation between fitted values and absolute residuals is clearly positive. This is what <code>het_breuschpagan</code> tests formally.</li></ol>
<figure><img src="images/lib18_ols.png" alt="Left: scatter with a fitted line. Right: residuals against fitted values forming a widening fan" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>A straight-line fit (left) and its residuals (right). The fan shape means uneven spread.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>How to read it:</strong> a healthy residual plot is a flat, even band around zero. Here the band widens to the right, so predictions at large x are less certain than the classic formula suggests. That is the cue to use robust errors, a weighted fit, or a transformed response.</p>
<h3>Worked example 3: the formula API and a GLM</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.9.3</span> Daily support tickets depend on the temperature and whether it is a weekend. Counts are not normal, so use a Poisson GLM. Formulas let you write the model like a sentence: statsmodels code below (<strong>not run here</strong>).</p>
@@NORUN
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import statsmodels.api as sm
import statsmodels.formula.api as smf

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
df = pd.DataFrame({"temp": rng.uniform(-5, 30, 300), "weekend": rng.integers(0, 2, 300)})
df["tickets"] = rng.poisson(np.exp(1.0 + 0.04 * df["temp"] - 0.5 * df["weekend"]))

ols = smf.ols("tickets ~ temp + C(weekend)", data=df).fit()          # formula API
glm = smf.glm("tickets ~ temp + weekend", data=df,
              family=sm.families.Poisson()).fit()
print(glm.summary())
print(np.exp(glm.params))          # rate ratios: multiplicative effects
@@END
<ol><li><code>smf.ols("tickets ~ temp + C(weekend)", data=df)</code> says "tickets depends on temp and the category weekend". <code>C(...)</code> turns a column into dummy variables. The intercept is added automatically by formulas.</li><li><code>family=sm.families.Poisson()</code> chooses the distribution of the response and, by default, a log link: the mean is exp(linear predictor).</li><li>Exponentiating the coefficients gives rate ratios: a coefficient of -0.5 on weekend means about 0.61 times as many tickets (e<sup>-0.5</sup>).</li></ol>
<p>The equivalent from first principles with SciPy (ran): maximise the Poisson log-likelihood, then take standard errors from the information matrix.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import optimize, stats

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
temp = rng.uniform(-5, 30, 300)
weekend = rng.integers(0, 2, 300)
tickets = rng.poisson(np.exp(1.0 + 0.04 * temp - 0.5 * weekend))
X = np.column_stack([np.ones(300), temp, weekend])

def nll(b):                                    # negative log-likelihood (constant dropped)
    eta = X @ b
    return np.sum(np.exp(eta) - tickets * eta)

res = optimize.minimize(nll, np.zeros(3), method="BFGS")
b = res.x
mu = np.exp(X @ b)
cov = np.linalg.inv(X.T @ (X * mu[:, None]))   # inverse Fisher information
se = np.sqrt(np.diag(cov))
z = b / se
for name, bi, si, zi in zip(["const", "temp", "weekend"], b, se, z):
    print("%-8s coef=%.3f se=%.3f z=%.1f p=%.3g" % (name, bi, si, zi, 2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(zi))))
print("rate ratio for weekend: %.2f" % np.exp(b[2]))
@@END
<ol><li>The truth is intercept 1.0, temperature 0.04, weekend -0.5. Counts come from <code>rng.poisson</code>.</li><li>For a Poisson model with a log link, the negative log-likelihood is the sum of exp(eta) minus y times eta, where eta is the linear predictor. Terms that do not depend on the coefficients are dropped.</li><li><code>minimize</code> finds the maximum-likelihood coefficients. This is the same objective statsmodels optimises (it uses a method called IRLS and arrives at the same maximum).</li><li>The covariance is the inverse of X<sup>T</sup> W X with W the fitted means; that is the Fisher information for a Poisson GLM with a log link. Standard errors and z-values follow.</li><li>The weekend rate ratio is exp of the weekend coefficient; the truth is e<sup>-0.5</sup>, about 0.61, and sampling noise explains the small gap.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 4: ARIMA, and an AR(1) computed by hand</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.9.4</span> A daily metric follows an AR(1) process: today = 0.7 times yesterday plus noise. Fit it and forecast the next 5 days. statsmodels (<strong>not run here</strong>):</p>
@@NORUN
import numpy as np
from statsmodels.tsa.arima.model import ARIMA

rng = np.random.default_rng(5)
y = np.zeros(300)
for i in range(1, 300):
    y[i] = 0.7 * y[i - 1] + rng.normal()

res = ARIMA(y, order=(1, 0, 0)).fit()        # (p, d, q) = (1, 0, 0), constant included
print(res.summary())
print(res.forecast(steps=5))
@@END
<ol><li><code>order=(p, d, q)</code> means p lagged values (AR), d differences to remove a trend, q lagged errors (MA). AR(1) is (1, 0, 0).</li><li><code>.fit()</code> estimates by maximum likelihood; the summary lists the AR coefficient and noise variance with standard errors.</li><li><code>.forecast(steps=5)</code> predicts the next five values, which decay toward the series mean.</li></ol>
<p>The same model, by regression on the lagged series (ran):</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(5)
y = np.zeros(300)
for i in range(1, 300):
    y[i] = 0.7 * y[i - 1] + rng.normal()

ybar = y.mean()
z = y - ybar
phi = (z[1:] @ z[:-1]) / (z[:-1] @ z[:-1])      # least-squares AR(1) coefficient
sigma = np.std(z[1:] - phi * z[:-1], ddof=1)
print("phi = %.3f  noise std = %.3f" % (phi, sigma))

forecast, last = [], z[-1]
for h in range(5):
    last = phi * last
    forecast.append(ybar + last)
print("forecast:", np.round(forecast, 3))
print("last observed value: %.3f, series mean: %.3f" % (y[-1], ybar))
@@END
<ol><li>The loop builds an AR(1) series: each value is 0.7 times the previous plus standard normal noise.</li><li>Subtract the mean, then regress today on yesterday through the origin; the coefficient is the sum of products over the sum of squares. It should be near the true 0.7.</li><li>The noise standard deviation is that of the one-step prediction errors.</li><li>Forecasting multiplies the last deviation by phi once per step, so the forecast shrinks geometrically toward the mean, exactly the decay you would see from <code>res.forecast</code>.</li></ol>
<div class="callout red"><div class="lab">COMMON MISTAKE</div><div class="txt">Running ARIMA on a series that trends or has a changing level without differencing. Check stationarity first (<code>adfuller</code>, or look at the plot), or set <code>d=1</code>. A model fitted to a trending series gives confident-looking but meaningless coefficients.</div></div>

<h2>18.10 matplotlib Quick Reference for Plotting Results</h2>
<p>This is only a cheat sheet for plotting the results in this chapter. The full visualisation guide (chart choice, design, storytelling) is in the Data Analytics document, Chapter 8. Always use the <strong>object-oriented style</strong>: <code>fig, ax = plt.subplots()</code>, then call methods on <code>ax</code>. It scales to many panels and avoids the confusion of hidden "current figure" state.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does in plain English</th><th>Minimal example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>plt.subplots(r, c, figsize)</code></td><td>Make a figure and a grid of axes</td><td><code>fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.plot(x, y)</code></td><td>Line</td><td><code>ax.plot(x, y, lw=2, label="fit")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.scatter(x, y)</code></td><td>Points, optionally sized or coloured</td><td><code>ax.scatter(x, y, s=20, c=z)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.hist(x, bins, density)</code></td><td>Histogram; <code>density=True</code> to compare with a pdf</td><td><code>ax.hist(x, bins=40, density=True)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.bar(x, h)</code></td><td>Bars for categories</td><td><code>ax.bar(names, values)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.errorbar</code></td><td>Points with error bars (estimates with CIs)</td><td><code>ax.errorbar(x, y, yerr=se, fmt="o")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.fill_between</code></td><td>Shaded band (uncertainty)</td><td><code>ax.fill_between(x, lo, hi, alpha=0.2)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.axhline / axvline</code></td><td>Reference line (zero, mean, threshold)</td><td><code>ax.axhline(0, color="black")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.contour / contourf</code></td><td>Level lines of a two-variable function</td><td><code>ax.contour(X, Y, Z)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.imshow</code></td><td>Matrix as an image (heat map)</td><td><code>ax.imshow(M, cmap="Blues")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.set_xlabel / set_ylabel / set_title</code></td><td>Label everything, always</td><td><code>ax.set_xlabel("hours")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.set_xscale("log")</code></td><td>Log axis for skewed or multiplicative data</td><td><code>ax.set_yscale("log")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.set_xlim / set_ylim</code></td><td>Zoom to a range</td><td><code>ax.set_xlim(0, 60)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.legend(frameon=False)</code></td><td>Show labels given in <code>label=</code></td><td><code>ax.legend()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ax.annotate</code></td><td>Text with an arrow at a point</td><td><code>ax.annotate("peak", (x0, y0), xytext=(x1, y1))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fig.tight_layout()</code></td><td>Stop labels overlapping</td><td><code>fig.tight_layout()</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fig.savefig(path, dpi)</code></td><td>Write PNG/SVG/PDF</td><td><code>fig.savefig("a.png", dpi=110)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>stats.probplot(x, plot=ax)</code></td><td>Q-Q plot for normality checks</td><td><code>stats.probplot(x, plot=ax)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>matplotlib.use("Agg")</code></td><td>Render without a screen (servers, scripts)</td><td><code>matplotlib.use("Agg")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>plt.close(fig)</code></td><td>Free memory in loops</td><td><code>plt.close(fig)</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.10.1</span> Plot a Q-Q plot, a histogram with the fitted density, and a means-with-intervals chart in one figure, then save it to memory.</p>
@@PY
import io
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from scipy import stats

rng = np.random.default_rng(7)
data = rng.lognormal(1.2, 0.5, 400)
s, loc, scale = stats.lognorm.fit(data, floc=0)

fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(12, 3.6))
stats.probplot(np.log(data), dist="norm", plot=axes[0])
axes[0].set_title("Q-Q plot of log(data)")

axes[1].hist(data, bins=30, density=True, alpha=0.5)
xs = np.linspace(0, data.max(), 200)
axes[1].plot(xs, stats.lognorm.pdf(xs, s, loc, scale), lw=2)
axes[1].set_title("histogram + fitted pdf")

groups = [rng.normal(m, 2, 30) for m in (10, 11, 13)]
means = [g.mean() for g in groups]
errs = [1.96 * stats.sem(g) for g in groups]
axes[2].errorbar([0, 1, 2], means, yerr=errs, fmt="o", capsize=4)
axes[2].set_xticks([0, 1, 2], ["A", "B", "C"])
axes[2].set_title("means with 95% CI")

fig.tight_layout()
buf = io.BytesIO()
fig.savefig(buf, format="png", dpi=80)
plt.close(fig)
print("panels:", len(axes), " png bytes > 5000:", buf.tell() > 5000)
print("x tick labels of last panel:", [t.get_text() for t in axes[2].get_xticklabels()])
@@END
<ol><li><code>plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(12, 3.6))</code> returns the figure and an array of three axes; all later calls go through <code>axes[i]</code>.</li><li><code>stats.probplot(..., plot=axes[0])</code> draws a Q-Q plot on that axis. Points on the straight line mean the data matches the distribution; here we test the <em>log</em> of the data against a normal.</li><li>The histogram uses <code>density=True</code> so its area is 1 and the fitted pdf can be overlaid on the same scale.</li><li><code>errorbar</code> draws a mean as a dot and 1.96 standard errors as whiskers. <code>set_xticks(positions, labels)</code> names the groups.</li><li>Saving to a <code>BytesIO</code> buffer is how you embed a figure in a web page or report without writing a file, and <code>plt.close(fig)</code> frees its memory.</li></ol>
